4.36.820.920 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\"status\":\"answered\",\"answer\":\"Double circulation refers to a system where blood passes through the heart twice during one complete cycle. According to the textbook, Harvey's observation and later studies revealed that blood first moves from the right ventricle to the lungs via arteries (called pulmonary circulation), where it picks up oxygen and then returns to the left atrium via veins. It then flows from the left ventricle to the rest of the body via the aorta (systemic circulation), before returning to the right atrium via veins. This process of blood reaching the heart twice for one complete circulation is what constitutes double circulation. This is vital in humans because it ensures that deoxygenated blood is efficiently sent to the lungs for oxygenation and oxygenated blood is effectively distributed to the body, facilitating the delivery of oxygen and nutrients to tissues and the removal of waste products. If the blood only circulated once, as initially thought, it would not be sufficient to meet the body's oxygen and nutrient requirements efficiently.\", \"selected_evidence_ids\":[\"E2\",\"E3\",\"E4\",\"E5\"],\"citations\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E2\",\"pdf_page\":67,\"textbook_page\":58},{\"evidence_id\":\"E3\",\"pdf_page\":72,\"textbook_page\":63},{\"evidence_id\":\"E4\",\"pdf_page\":83,\"textbook_page\":74},{\"evidence_id\":\"E5\",\"pdf_page\":84,\"textbook_page\":75}],\"missing_information\":[]}\n```"}
